description.objectives:
Students acquire a unified view of graph algorithms, sparse linear algebra, and graph learning. They learn to formulate graph computations as sparse operators, to analyze their algorithmic and architectural properties, and to develop scalable implementations for modern parallel systems. After successful completion of the module, students are able to

analyze large-scale graph and sparse matrix problems and identify suitable algorithmic abstractions for their solution;

formulate graph algorithms using sparse linear algebra concepts and relate classical graph algorithms to sparse computational kernels;

evaluate algorithmic trade-offs with respect to computational complexity, memory consumption, communication requirements, and scalability;

design and implement efficient graph and sparse matrix algorithms on modern parallel architectures;

apply advanced techniques such as spectral methods, graph coarsening, sparsification, and multilevel approaches to reduce computational complexity and improve scalability;

assess the impact of memory hierarchies, communication costs, and load balancing on algorithm performance and derive suitable optimization strategies;

explain graph neural networks and related graph learning methods as parameterized sparse propagation operators and assess their computational characteristics;

transfer algorithmic concepts and implementation techniques to new sparse data models

After a successful project, the students can:

select and apply modern machine learning methods to solve a data science problem;

organize themselves in a team in a goal-oriented manner and bring an extensive software project in the field of data science and machine learning to success;

deepen their data science and machine learning skills

solve a finance problem with the help of data science and machine learning algorithm.
